# Two things about this application cannot be arranged by a package: a group membership and,
# on some desktops, a second typing tool. Both are silent failures at the moment they matter -
# a key that never arrives, a sentence that is never typed - so they are said here, once,
# where a person is still looking at the terminal.

post_install() {
    cat <<'MESSAGE'

OpenRamble reads the dictation key straight from /dev/input: no Wayland protocol
reports a key being held, so there is nothing else to read it from. That needs
the "input" group -

    sudo gpasswd -a "$USER" input

- and a fresh login for it to take effect. Without it the application starts,
says so in the tray, and never sees the key.

If joining that group is not an option, your compositor can drive the key
instead: it runs a command on the key's press and release, and that command talks
to a socket the application always listens on -

    echo status | socat -t1 - UNIX-CONNECT:$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/openramble/ctl.sock

Install socat for it (it is ~1 ms per keypress against ~20 ms for the built-in
"openramble ctl hotkey press" client, and that difference lands on the release
edge). Ready-made, commented binds for both hold and toggle:

    /usr/share/openramble/hyprland-example.conf

Typing into other people's windows goes through wtype, which needs the
compositor's virtual-keyboard protocol: Sway, Hyprland, KDE and the wlroots
desktops have it, GNOME and X11 sessions do not. There, install ydotool and let
its daemon run:

    sudo pacman -S ydotool
    systemctl --user enable --now ydotool

ydotool types through /dev/uinput. If its daemon cannot open that, the usual
remedy is /etc/udev/rules.d/99-uinput.rules -

    KERNEL=="uinput", GROUP="input", MODE="0660", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"

- followed by: sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo modprobe uinput

The recognition model (~720 MB) is not in this package. The application
downloads it on first launch, and that download is the only network access it
has: no audio and no text ever leaves the machine.

MESSAGE
}

post_upgrade() {
    post_install
}
